      <!-- Section: Signs Your Child May Be Ready for Violin Lessons -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">Signs Your Child May Be Ready for Violin Lessons</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Instead of looking for a strict age cutoff, it helps to pay attention to readiness signals. Your child may be ready to explore lessons if they show some combination of these:</p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 pl-5 list-none">
        {[
          "They are curious about the violin or music.",
          "They can engage with another adult for short periods with appropriate support.",
          "They are willing to imitate, experiment, or try something more than once.",
          "They can tolerate small amounts of frustration without every difficult moment ending the activity.",
          "They enjoy listening to music, making sounds, moving to rhythm, or exploring instruments.",
          "For younger children, a parent or caregiver is willing to participate in establishing simple routines at home.",
        ].map(item => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-base text-ink/70 font-light leading-relaxed">
            <span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-purplePrimary opacity-60 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">None of these needs to be perfect. Readiness is not the same thing as already knowing how to be a violin student. Learning how to learn the instrument is part of what lessons are for.</p>

      <!-- Section: Your Child Does Not Need to Show "Talent" Before Starting -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">Your Child Does Not Need to Show "Talent" Before Starting</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Natural talent isn't a useful test for whether a child should begin. Beginners are beginners.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Coordination can be learned. Listening develops. Musical understanding grows. Focus changes with experience. Children become more capable by doing things they could not already do.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">What matters much more is whether the learning environment gives a child appropriate challenges, useful feedback, room to make mistakes, and reasons to stay curious. A child does not need to prove that they deserve music lessons by already being good at music.</p>

      <!-- Mid-article CTA -->
      <div class="mt-10 flex">
        <a href="/about" class="btn-secondary" aria-label="Learn about Rae's teaching approach at Vibrations Violin Studio">
          Learn About Rae's Teaching Approach
        </a>
